2026-02-01 23:33:32,173 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Limitations of NPV

A notable limitation of NPV analysis is that it makes assumptions about future events that may not prove correct. The discount rate value used is a judgment call, while the cost of an investment and its projected returns are necessarily estimates. The NPV calculation is only as reliable as its underlying assumptions.

Also, the NPV formula yields a dollar result that, though easy to interpret, may not tell the entire story. Consider the following two investment options: Option A with an NPV of \$100,000, or Option B with an NPV of \$1,000. 
But how about if Option A requires an initial investment of \$1 million, while Option B will only cost \$10? The extreme numbers in the example make a point. 
The NPV formula doesn’t evaluate a project’s return on investment (ROI), a key consideration for anyone with finite capital. Though the NPV formula estimates how much value a project will produce, it doesn’t show if it's an efficient use of your investment dollars. is relevant
