2026-02-01 23:33:35,679 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Explain Like I'm 5

Return on assets (ROA) is a method of measuring how well a company uses what it owns, like buildings and equipment, to make money. It can be viewed as a way to see how much profit a company earns for every dollar it has in assets.

If a company's ROA is high, it is doing a good job using its assets to make money. For example, if a company has an ROA of 25%, it is generating 25 cents for every dollar of its assets.

If it has a low ROA, then it isn't effectively generating enough money on the assets it owns. For example, if it has an ROA of 5%, it is only earning 5 cents for every dollar of its assets. is relevant
